-// OutboundProxy allows only operator-declared outbound hosts (declared-outbound
-// permission). v1 is proxy-cooperation: the host enforces the allowlist for
-// calls routed through it. A native worker retains direct socket access, so OS
-// network confinement is deferred (see optimizations.md). The proxy method
-// honors the allowlist; that is what v1 tests assert.
+// OutboundRequest is one outbound HTTP call a worker asks the Host to perform on
+// its behalf (declared-outbound permission). Pure data: the worker never dials a
+// socket through this path — the Host carries the traffic. Method/URL/Header/Body
+// are the request shape; the proxy implementation validates them against the
+// plugin's declared allowlist and the SSRF contract below before dialing.
+type OutboundRequest struct {
+	Method string
+	URL    string
+	Header map[string][]string
+	Body   []byte
+}
+
+// OutboundResponse is the Host's reply for a carried outbound call. Pure data.
+type OutboundResponse struct {
+	StatusCode int
+	Header     map[string][]string
+	Body       []byte
+}
+
+// OutboundProxy carries operator-declared outbound HTTP for a worker
+// (declared-outbound permission). Unlike the removed advisory ask-then-dial
+// method, the worker never opens its own socket through this path: it hands the
+// Host an OutboundRequest and the Host performs the fetch. Once a real adapter is
+// wired, outbound is therefore enforced by construction, not cooperation. The
+// seam is nil-by-default (dormant ⇒ permission-denied), exactly like the
+// ingest/file-mutation/jobs Core-handoff seams, because the declared-outbound
+// allowlist source does not exist yet (see the re-ticketed follow-ups).
+//
+// ADR-0012 §9 SSRF contract (binding obligation of any RequestOutbound adapter).
+// On every request, including after each redirect, the enforced proxy MUST:
+//   - pin scheme+host to the plugin's declared-outbound allowlist and fail closed
+//     if the resolved host is not declared;
+//   - deny private, link-local, loopback, and cloud-metadata (169.254.169.254) IP
+//     ranges;
+//   - use a DNS-rebinding-safe dialer (resolve once, then dial the vetted IP);
+//   - refuse cross-host redirects; and
+//   - apply time and response-size caps.
 //
-// TODO(PLUG-3): OS-level network confinement for native workers is currently
-// advisory-only — a worker can bypass the allowlist via direct socket access.
-// Remediation path: implement OS sandbox confinement (e.g. Linux seccomp-BPF
-// network filter or macOS sandbox profile) that blocks all outbound sockets
-// except those the host explicitly proxies, making declared-outbound
+// This ticket delivers the seam shape, the Host enforcement gate, and this
+// contract as the seam's documented obligation and interface; the concrete
+// hardened http.Client adapter (and the allowlist source it needs) are re-ticketed
+// follow-ups. Until they land, declared-outbound is grantable but first-party-
+// trusted-only for execution (see FirstPartyTrust and Host.RequestOutbound).
+//
+// TODO(PLUG-3): OS-level network confinement for native workers is separate and
+// still deferred — a native worker retains direct OS socket access outside this
+// proxy path. Remediation path: implement OS sandbox confinement (e.g. Linux
+// seccomp-BPF network filter or macOS sandbox profile) that blocks all outbound
+// sockets except those the host explicitly proxies, making declared-outbound
 // enforcement hard rather than cooperative. Track as a post-v1 security
 // hardening item; gate removal of this TODO on a passing OS-confinement
 // integration test for each supported platform.
 type OutboundProxy interface {
-	// AllowOutbound reports whether host is in the plugin's declared outbound
-	// allowlist. The actual request is made by the host on the worker's behalf.
-	AllowOutbound(pluginID, host string) bool
+	// RequestOutbound performs the fetch on the worker's behalf, enforcing the
+	// declared allowlist and the ADR-0012 §9 SSRF contract above server-side. The
+	// worker never dials directly through this path.
+	RequestOutbound(ctx context.Context, pluginID string, req OutboundRequest) (OutboundResponse, error)
+}
+
+// FirstPartyTrust reports whether a plugin is a first-party Integration Worker.
+// It is the injectable source of truth the outbound-execution gate consults: an
+// operator may still grant declared-outbound to any plugin (ADR-0058 §29), but
+// carrying arbitrary outbound traffic safely requires the SSRF-hardened executor,
+// which exists only for first-party plugins for now. So Host.RequestOutbound
+// executes only for a first-party-trusted plugin until that executor lands. The
+// production wiring supplies an empty set (no first-party Integration Worker ships
+// in v1) ⇒ every third-party plugin is refused; the source is injectable so a
+// first-party plugin (or a test) can actually carry outbound.
+type FirstPartyTrust interface {
+	IsFirstParty(pluginID string) bool
 }
+
+// TrustSet is a set of first-party plugin ids implementing FirstPartyTrust. The
+// empty (or nil) set denies all — the fail-closed production posture.
+type TrustSet map[string]bool
+
+// IsFirstParty reports whether pluginID is in the trusted set. Empty/nil ⇒ false.
+func (t TrustSet) IsFirstParty(pluginID string) bool { return t[pluginID] }
